Here are some steps you can follow to fix the issue of Facebook not picking up an image when sharing your WordPress blog post:

1. Check if there is an Open Graph meta tag for the image:
   - Go to your WordPress dashboard and click on Appearance > Customize.
   - Select the "Sharing" option from the customizer menu.
   - Make sure that the "Show Title and Description" and "Show Image" options are enabled.
   - In the "Image Source" section, enter the URL of the image you want to use as the featured image.
   - Save your changes and preview your page to see if Facebook is now displaying the image in the preview.
2. Use a plugin:
   - There are several plugins available that can help with optimizing your WordPress site for social media, such as Yoast SEO or All in One SEO Pack.
   - These plugins will allow you to add Open Graph meta tags directly from the WordPress dashboard.
3. Add the Open Graph meta tags manually:
   - If you prefer to add the Open Graph meta tags manually, you can do so by adding the following code to your header.php file:
   ```
   <?php
   function add_facebook_opengraph() {
    echo '<meta property="og:title" content="' . Get_the_title() . '" />';
    echo '<meta property="og:type" content="article" />';
    echo '<meta property="og:url" content="' . Get_permalink() . '" />';
    echo '<meta property="og:image" content="' . Get_the_post_thumbnail_url(get_the_ID(), 'full') . '" />';
    echo '<meta property="og:description" content="' . Get_the_excerpt() . '" />';
    echo '<meta property="og:site_name" content="Your Site Name" />';
   }
   add_action('wp_head', 'add_facebook_opengraph');
   ```
   - Replace "Your Site Name" with your actual site name.

After implementing any of these solutions, clear your browser cache and try sharing your blog post again on Facebook to see if the issue has been resolved.

To create a function in JavaScript that sends a message to a Slack channel using the Slack API, follow these steps:

1. First, you need to sign up for a Slack API developer account and generate a bot token. This token will be used to authenticate your application with the Slack API.

2. Next, install the `axios` library by running `npm install axios` in your terminal or command prompt. This library is used to make HTTP requests.

3. Create a new file (e.g., `slack-message.js`) and define your function as follows:

```javascript
const axios = require('axios');

function sendSlackMessage(text, channel) {
  const webhookUrl = 'https://hooks.slack.com/services/' + process.env.SLACK_BOT_TOKEN + '/' + process.env.SLACK_APP_ID + '/' + channel;

  const data = {
    text: text
  };

  return axios.post(webhookUrl, data);
}

// Example usage
sendSlackMessage('This is a test message.', '#general').then((response) => {
  console.log('Slack message sent successfully.');
}).catch((error) => {
  console.log('Error sending Slack message:', error);
});
```

In this example, the `sendSlackMessage()` function takes two arguments - `text` and `channel`. The `text` parameter contains the message you want to send, and the `channel` parameter specifies the channel where you want to post the message.

The function first retrieves the webhook URL for the specified channel using the Slack API. It then creates a `data` object containing the message text and makes an HTTP POST request to the webhook URL using the `axios.post()` method.

The returned promise can be handled using the `then()` and `catch()` methods to handle successful and failed responses, respectively.

Make sure to replace the placeholders `SLACK_BOT_TOKEN` and `SLACK_APP_ID` in the webhook URL with your actual values obtained from the Slack API dashboard. Also, remember to set these environment variables securely.

