To add a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can create a Gaussian kernel using the `fspecial` function in MATLAB, which generates specialized filters based on mathematical functions. Here's how you can modify your existing code:

```matlab
% Load input image
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
% Convert to grayscale
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Create Gaussian filter with standard deviation of 3 pixels
gaussian_kernel = fspecial('gaussian', [3 3], 3);
% Apply Gaussian filter to input image
filtered_image = filter2(double(gray_image), gaussian_kernel, 'replicate');
% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator to filtered image in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');
% Apply Sobel operator to filtered image in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions and display result
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this modified code, we first create a Gaussian kernel using the `fspecial` function with a standard deviation of 3 pixels. We then apply this filter to the input image using the `filter2` function, which returns a filtered version of the image. After applying the Gaussian filter, we proceed with defining the Sobel operators and applying them to the filtered image as before. The resulting edge image will have reduced noise due to the Gaussian filtering step.

