To make the circle element look more 3D using CSS, you can apply some additional styles to it. Here's an updated version of the `.circle` selector that incorporates some 3D effects:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray) brightness(0.9);
  perspective: 800px; /* Add this property */
  transform-style: preserve-3d; /* Add this property */
  transform: translateZ(0) scale(1); /* Add these properties */
  transition: transform 0.3s ease-in-out;
  background: linear-gradient(to bottom, #fff, #ffcccc);
  box-shadow: 10px 10px 5px 0px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
  border-radius: 70%;
}

/* For hover effect */
.circle:hover {
  transform: translateZ(10px) scale(1.1);
}
```

Let me explain what each added property does:

1. Perspective: This sets the distance between the camera and the plane where the circle is located. The larger the value, the farther away the circle appears, which adds depth.

2. Transform-style: This specifies how children elements are transformed in relation to their parent. In our case, we want to preserve the 3D space, so we set this to `preserve-3d`.

3. Transform: This applies the actual transformation to the circle element. We're translating it along the z-axis (depth) by 0 pixels (no translation), scaling it by 1 (no change), and applying a hover effect that moves it back along the z-axis by 10 pixels (deeper) and scales it up by 10% (larger).

These changes will give your circle a more 3D appearance when viewed on a modern browser that supports the WebGL rendering context.

