```java
import java.util.InputMismatchException;
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        int num = -1; // initialize variable with default value

        while (true) {
            System.out.print("Enter a number: ");

            try {
                num = scanner.nextInt(); // attempt to read integer from user input
                break; // exit loop if integer is successfully read
            } catch (InputMismatchException e) {
                System.out.println("Invalid input! Please enter an integer."); // handle InputMismatchException by printing error message and clearing input buffer
                scanner.nextLine(); // consume non-integer tokens in input buffer to prevent infinite loop
            }
        }

        System.out.println("You entered: " + num); // print user input
    }
}
```

In this modified version of your code, I've added a `while` loop around the user input prompt to create a simple input validation mechanism. The loop will continue running until the user enters a valid integer. Inside the loop, I'm wrapping the call to `scanner.nextInt()` inside a `try` block to catch any potential `InputMismatchException` thrown by `Scanner`. If such an exception is caught, it prints an error message and consumes any remaining non-integer tokens in the input buffer using `scanner.nextLine()`, which prevents an infinite loop from occurring. Once a valid integer is successfully read, the loop exits and the program continues executing as before. This approach helps to improve the robustness and usability of your program by providing clearer feedback to the user in case of invalid input.

